 ## Background
-The problem of joint pose and NeRF optimization emerged as a solution to the dependency on accurate Structure-from-Motion (SfM) pipelines, which can fail on textureless or repetitive surfaces [G]. Early work, **NeRF–** (February 2021), jointly estimated NeRF model parameters and camera parameters, achieving comparable results to COLMAP-based baselines but struggling with rotational motion [S1]. Concurrently, **BARF** (April 2021) introduced coarse-to-fine registration, outperforming NeRF– on LLFF forward-facing scenes [S1]. **SCNeRF** (August 2021) extended this to self-calibration, optimizing intrinsic parameters for non-linear models like fisheye lenses using curriculum learning [S1]. **iNeRF** (December 2020) formulated pose reconstruction as an inverse problem given a pre-trained NeRF, using photo-metric loss and interest-point sampling [S1].
+The problem of joint pose and NeRF optimization emerged as a solution to the dependency on accurate Structure-from-Motion (SfM) pipelines, which can fail in low-texture or dynamic scenes. Early work by Yen-Chen et al. (iNeRF, December 2020) formulated pose reconstruction as an inverse problem, optimizing pose using photometric loss on a pre-trained NeRF [S1]. Subsequently, NeRF– (February 2021) jointly estimated NeRF model parameters and camera parameters, allowing for end-to-end radiance field construction comparable to COLMAP baselines [S1]. Jeong et al. introduced SCNeRF (August 2021), which extended this to self-calibration of intrinsic parameters for non-linear models like fisheye lenses using curriculum learning [S1]. These methods collectively established the foundation for differentiable inverse rendering where camera parameters are treated as learnable variables alongside scene geometry.
 
